\f1\fs22 \'91Terrorist activity, eleven o\'92clock, three hundred metres,\'92 the pilot called.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Damn! The ambulances are already there.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Apparently delta squad\'92s roadblocks failed, sir,\'92 a riot cop reported to base.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Copy, alpha squad. You\'92re cleared to continue the operation around the ambulance crews.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander could contain his entitled agency no longer. His role, as ever, was to lead, and make a difference. He strode to the cruiser\'92s cockpit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir,\'92 the co-pilot said. \'91You\'92re safer in the cabin.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m in charge here, captain,\'92 Alexander told the co-pilot.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, you are,\'92said a voice of authority from the cabin. \'91For as long as you give no orders.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander puffed up with defiance until he saw the cop\'97the true leader of the operation\'97would call his bluff. \'91I want see what\'92s going on,\'92 was all he could summon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, buckle up then,\'92 the pilot ordered. \'91If we have to take evasive manoeuvres, I don\'92t want your pussy arse landing in my lap.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander took a second-row seat and tried his best to peer out the windows.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The face of a building burned and crumbled. Fire and smoke issued from the ground floor. The blast radius was substantial. Buildings across the street stood blackened and aghast. The bomb blew in windows for a hundred metres around, scorched the street and set fire to ground cars. A few stragglers limped or crawled from the explosion. Many lay writhing on street, burned and broken. From Alexander\'92s position, the motionless ones looked like crushed beetles. He saw very little blood, only ravaged clothing and injuries cauterised or caked in ash.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Less than a pace back from the ring scorching the street, a crowd of gawkers swelled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s nowhere to land,\'92 the co-pilot reported. \'91Other than right on top of the injured. And ambulances.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Copy,\'92 answered Captain Junkers, the cop-in-charge, and considered landing exactly where the fuck he wanted. If a few ambulances had to scatter, and he crushed a few soon-to-be-dead, so be it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir,\'92 the cop with the sniper gun said. \'91I can cover it, sir.\'92\
