\f1\fs22 The riot cops flung open the cabin doors as the pilot lowered the cruiser over the crush in the street. Pistols withdrew from holsters and aimed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Only take out enough to set them moving,\'92 Corporal Sman ordered. \'91I don\'92t fancy being here all night to balance the books.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman\'92s colleagues, who often accused him of pleasuring himself by creating pivot tables (they were clueless about their function), promptly emptied their magazines into the gawking throng. Sman swore.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The reaction was instant. Shrieks and howls pierced the air. The crowd undulated like snakes beneath a quilt, surging one way and then the other. Eventually, most of the crowd voted with scampering feet, leaving behind a grisly crop circle of trampled bodies.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the co-pilot landed the cruiser, all the riot cops jumped out, except Sman who parked his butt in the port doorway and dangled his legs outside. The cruiser rose again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We need the right demographic spread of terrorists. Make sure you stick to the profiles I specified,\'92 Sman ordered as he practiced his aim.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Roger that,\'92 the Junkers answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander came and stood by Sman, and grabbed the doorway to keep balance as the cruiser slowly rotated. \'91How many more do you have to take out?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman threw a panoramic hologram into the air outside the cruiser, where it would be easy for him to monitor. Statistical tables scrolled, charts and graphs materialised, and faces appeared one after the other. \'91Less than I\'92d like,\'92 he answered, aimed and shot into the crowd. A face popped up on his hologram with a green tick animating over it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91So I\'92m just here to endorse the culling of so-called terrorists,\'92 Alexander said sullenly. It was not that he objected to the killing; it galled that he was not in charge of it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman took another shot. The hologram produced another face and another green tick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cops punched through the crowd, looking for arrests that matched their profiles. The cruiser lazily rotated as Sman found targets and fired. On his hologram, a graph showed a series of lines that seems to be converging, and a pair of pie charts that were near becoming exact copies.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Suddenly, the graphs lost their cohesion. Sman swore. \'91What\'92s going on down there?\'92 he demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92ve had some Resist Arrests,\'92 Junkers reported from the ground. \'91Kramer shot a banker.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman was furious. \'91Now the demographic analysis looks like the banker was our sole intended target! I\'92ll have to start over.\'92 Sman twiddled and punched at the hologram and the whole of its panorama refreshed. Sman took aim and smiled at something in his scope. \'91Ah! White hip hop artist!\'92 He pulled the trigger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They\'92re a needed demographic here?\'92 Alexander asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Not exactly. But, you can never take out too many white hip hop artists.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Aren\'92t you endangering the demographic balance of the whole operation?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nah,\'92 Sman said as he popped more targets and earned more green ticks. \'91Community service. See?\'92 He paused and pointed to a floating graph. \'91Even the demographics don\'92t care.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91All targets acquired,\'92 Junkers reported.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pilot landed the cruiser. The cops loaded a restrained, gagged and blindfolded mess of terrorists into the cargo hold, and boarded the cruiser.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Once in the air, Sman resumed shooting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Almost there?\'92 Junkers quizzed him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman shot a few times then consulted his charts and graphs. \'91Pretty much, yeah. I could do with one more, just to be safe. Have the pilot hover over the blast site.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What are you looking for?\'92 Alexander asked, hoping he could be helpful, so he could tells his friends later, his contribution was the clincher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I need an average Joe, preferably one of the bomb victims. White, thirties, manual labourer \'85 you know, a gardener or a cleaner or something.\'92 Sman scrolled through the casualty reports. the hologram went 
\f3\i bing
\f1\i0  and produced a face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>To Alexander\'92s horror, it was Tony\'92s. Jesus, they\'92d only been chatting last night, when Alexander called him in to clean up that suicide mess. With wide, panicked eyes, he scoured the blast zone for any sign of his \'85 well, not a friend exactly\'97Tony was Cloudborne, after all\'97more a compatriot in a loosely acquainted kind of way (had a beer with him once or twice).\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when Alexander saw her. She was lying dead or unconscious in the street, black and grey, cut and bruised. The way she lay was mesmerising. The flow of her long ashen hair spoke of the pure blonde beneath the caking dust. The soot could not disguise the architecture of her cheek bones, nor the artistry of the shape of her lips. Even her body lying at misshapen angles could not disguise the elegance of her legs, the suppleness of her hips, the promise of her breasts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Choirs of angels sang to Alexander, and he fell in love.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At which time the memory of Sman\'92s words intruded \'85 
\f3\i white, thirties, manual labourer \'85 a gardener or a cleaner or something
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Next to the object of Alexander\'92s adulation, a charcoaled lump of a man, a carcass, lay. 
\f3\i Who is he, who lies so near my angel in tragedy? 
\f1\i0 He was cut up and bruised, almost as much as the angel, his hair had mostly frizzled away. Something about the angel\'92s companion poked at Alexander\'92s attention, and irritated it into recognition. 
\f3\i Tony?
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Found him!\'92 Sman smiled with the satisfaction of the thrill of a chase nearing its fruition. He trained his rifle down and steadied his aim.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander kicked Sman\'92s rifle. The particle beam went wide and blew out an ambulance tyre. Alexander kicked again. The rifle clattered on the cruiser\'92s landing skids and fell to the street. In a second, Junkers was standing chest-to-chest with Alexander. \'91What in the Alfred Deakin was that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sorry.\'92 Alexander fumbled. \'91Sciatic tic. Old public relations injury.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the actual tax, lieutenant?\'92 Junkers blustered, and handed Sman his pistol. \'91Take the shot, Corporal.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes. sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You can\'92t shoot him.\'92 Alexander was suddenly assertive. Well, partly assertive, mostly pleading.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And just why the Edmund Barton not, Lieutenant?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander worked his jaw and compressed his lips. He couldn\'92t admit to knowing Tony, certainly not in this situation. \'91Take them to a hospital,\'92 Alexander said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Junkers\'92 eyes bulged. \'91I knew bringing you on this mission was a bad idea. Take the shot, Corporal.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman aimed the pistol. Alexander surprised Junkers, and shoved him off his feet. Before Sman could steady his aim, Alexander planted his foot between his shoulder blades and assisted him out of the cruiser. Sman died with an arrested wail in his throat, his brains dashed out on smouldering asphalt.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Junkers waved the others back from reacting, wiped his mouth and considered how he\'92d end this showtime impersonator.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You know,\'92 Alexander said, \'91for the sake of demographics, given the way the corporal mopped up after your grunts, we could classify him as a cleaner, couldn\'92t we?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A smile cracked Junkers\'92 face. \'91Son of a sow,\'92 he said, seeing Alexander as if for the first time.
